window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][20]={"university_physics_en:M20-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M20-L1 – Astrometry, Parallax and the Distance Ladder<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Bachelor: Astrophysics and Cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7: geometrical optics; M16:\nmeasurement uncertainty; M19: model criticism. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson: Computational\nRecords and Convergence<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Infer a distance from parallax\nand distinguish geometric from photometric distance estimates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Stellar distances cannot be measured with an ordinary ruler. Instead,\nobservers use the apparent displacement of a nearby star against very\ndistant background sources. Earth’s orbital motion provides a known\nbaseline. Stellar parallax is <strong>half<\/strong> the angular\ndisplacement between idealised observations roughly six months apart.\nThis geometric method does not require knowing a star’s luminosity.\nApparent brightness, by contrast, combines intrinsic luminosity,\ndistance and possible attenuation by dust.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For small angles, the parsec is defined by\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d[\\mathrm{pc}]=\\frac{1}{p[\\mathrm{arcsec}]},<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes annual trigonometric parallax in arcseconds. One arcsecond is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3600<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/3600<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof a degree; the definition uses a baseline of one astronomical unit.\nFor isotropic emission without attenuation, the measured flux is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=L/(4\\pi d^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nApparent magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand absolute magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nobey\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m-M=5\\log_{10}\\!\\left(\\frac{d}{10\\,\\mathrm{pc}}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nThis elementary relation presumes compatible photometric definitions and\nnegligible extinction. Real measurements also require treatment of\nsystematic angular offsets and statistical uncertainty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>A synthetic measurement yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>0.050<\/mn><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.050&#39;&#39;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe geometric distance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=20\\,\\mathrm{pc}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor apparent magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8.00<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=8.00<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe distance modulus is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.505<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\log_{10}(20/10)=1.505<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6.49<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\approx6.49<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif extinction is ignored. The diagram shows the parallax geometry and\nthe inverse angular-distance relation; angles are deliberately not drawn\nto scale.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_280_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Parallax triangle between two Earth positions and a nearby star, with a parallax-versus-distance curve.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Parallax triangle between two Earth\npositions and a nearby star, with a parallax-versus-distance\ncurve.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Geometric parallax and inverse relation between distance and\nangle (schematic).<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A parallax of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.10\\,\\mathrm{arcsec}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured. What is the distance?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the distance modulus for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100\\,\\mathrm{pc}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does observed flux change if distance doubles while luminosity\nis fixed?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.10<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=1/0.10=10\\,\\mathrm{pc}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m-M=5\\log_{10}(10)=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfalls to one quarter because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F\\propto d^{-2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>When parallax uncertainty is comparable to its measured value, naive\ninversion can bias distances; appropriate inference uses a statistical\nmodel and, where justified, prior information on stellar populations.\nExtinction and wavelength dependence can modify the magnitude relation.\nMore distant galaxies require further methods in a distance ladder;\nparallax by itself is insufficient. The graphic is an original schematic\nteaching illustration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Parallax provides a geometric distance; brightness-based methods\nsupplement it with astrophysical assumptions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Stellar Spectra, Temperature and\nRadial Velocity<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/19-2-surveying-the-stars\">OpenStax – Astronomy 2e, §19.2 Surveying the Stars<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Parallax/Triangulation, §19.2: parallax geometry; Subject relevance to lesson: M20-L1 – Astrometry, Parallax and the Distance Ladder · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M20-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M20-L2 – Stellar Spectra, Temperature and Radial Velocity<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Bachelor: Astrophysics and Cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7: spectroscopy; M10:\nelectromagnetic waves; M11: atomic transitions. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson: Astrometry,\nParallax and the Distance Ladder<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Use spectral signatures to\nestimate temperature and radial motion within the model assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>A star is not a monochromatic source. Its continuum depends strongly\non temperature and radiation transport, while absorption and sometimes\nemission lines are superimposed. Line profiles and strengths respond to\ncomposition, excitation, ionisation, surface gravity and motion.\nSpectral classes O, B, A, F, G, K and M broadly order stars by\ntemperature-sensitive spectral signatures. An isolated feature is not an\nunambiguous chemical fingerprint without an atmosphere model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For an ideal blackbody, Wien’s displacement law gives the wavelength\nof maximum spectral radiance per wavelength interval:\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>b<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.898<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{\\max}T=b,\\qquad b\\approx2.898\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{m\\,K}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nFor radial speeds small compared with the speed of light, a\nspectral-line shift satisfies\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mi>c<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\Delta\\lambda}{\\lambda_0}\\approx\\frac{v_r}{c}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nPositive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes recession and hence a redshift in this convention. At high\nspeeds, or with gravitational or cosmological shifts, this\nnon-relativistic Doppler approximation is insufficient. Blackbody peaks\nin wavelength and frequency representations do not occur at\ncorresponding coordinates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>At an effective temperature of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5800<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5800\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe ideal wavelength peak is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{\\max}\\approx500\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSuppose a synthetic line has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>656.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_0=656.3\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>656.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{\\mathrm{obs}}=656.5\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>656.3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>91.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_r\\approx c(0.2/656.3)\\approx91.4\\,\\mathrm{km/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram compares two normalised thermal spectra and a model\nabsorption line; it is not a set of measured data.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_282_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two calculated blackbody spectra at different temperatures and a schematic absorption line.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two calculated blackbody spectra at\ndifferent temperatures and a schematic absorption line.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Thermal spectra at 3500 K and 7000 K, with a model spectral\nline.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{\\max}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=4000\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A line at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>656.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">656.3\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis observed at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>656.9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">656.9\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEstimate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does line strength alone not uniquely measure elemental\nabundance?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>725<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{\\max}\\approx725\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>656.3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>274<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_r\\approx c(0.6/656.3)\\approx274\\,\\mathrm{km/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(receding).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Excitation, ionisation, pressure and radiative transfer also control\nline strength.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>Wien’s law provides a characteristic temperature, not a complete\nstellar-atmosphere model. Radial velocities require multiple spectral\nfeatures and calibration against instrumental drift. Inferring redshift\nand abundance is therefore a model-based process rather than a simple\nreading of colour or one line.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Spectroscopy connects thermal continua, atomic lines and motion, but\nit requires explicit modelling assumptions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Stellar Structure, Hydrostatic\nEquilibrium and Evolution<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/17-3-the-spectra-of-stars-and-brown-dwarfs\">OpenStax – Astronomy 2e, §17.3 The Spectra of Stars (and Brown Dwarfs)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Spectral classes and atmospheric lines, §17.3: spectral classes and lines; Subject relevance to lesson: M20-L2 – Stellar Spectra, Temperature and Radial Velocity · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M20-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M20-L3 – Stellar Structure, Hydrostatic Equilibrium and Evolution<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Bachelor: Astrophysics and Cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M3: gravitation; M5: thermodynamics;\nM13: equations of state. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson: Stellar\nSpectra, Temperature and Radial Velocity<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive mass conservation and\nhydrostatic equilibrium for a stellar model and explain their\nlimitations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Why does a star not instantly collapse under its own gravity? During\napproximately stationary phases of evolution, inward gravity is balanced\nby an outward pressure force arising from a pressure that decreases with\nradius. A star is a stratified fluid rather than a solid shell. In many\nmain-sequence stars, nuclear reactions supply energy that moves outward\nthrough radiation diffusion or convection. Whether a stellar object\nsatisfies the static approximation depends on its evolutionary\nstage.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>Assume spherical symmetry and let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m(r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the mass enclosed by radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mass of a thin spherical shell implies\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm dm}{\\mathrm dr}=4\\pi r^2\\rho(r).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nHydrostatic force balance then gives\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\mathrm dP}{\\mathrm dr}=-\\frac{Gm(r)\\rho(r)}{r^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the local energy generation rate per unit mass, a highly simplified\nstationary model obeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm dL/\\mathrm dr=4\\pi r^2\\rho\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen other energy sources or sinks can be neglected. Combined with an\nequation of state and energy-transport equations, these expressions form\nthe basis of stellar structure models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>For a hypothetical constant-density sphere,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m(r)=4\\pi\\rho r^3/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIntegrating hydrostatic balance subject to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(R)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><msup><mi>ρ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(r)=2\\pi G\\rho^2(R^2-r^2)/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence the central pressure is\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>G<\/mi><msup><mi>M<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_c=\\frac{3GM^2}{8\\pi R^4}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>30<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=1.0\\times10^{30}\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=5.0\\times10^8\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.27<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>14<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_c\\approx1.27\\times10^{14}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is <strong>not<\/strong> a realistic solar model: actual density\nvaries strongly with radius. The diagram depicts model layers and a\nschematic pressure profile.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_283_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic stellar cross-section with core, radiative and convective zones, plus declining pressure profile.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic stellar cross-section with\ncore, radiative and convective zones, plus declining pressure\nprofile.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Idealised stellar layers and decreasing pressure versus radius;\nnot an observed star.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Derive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m(r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor uniform density.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if radius is halved at fixed mass?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">State two assumptions behind the hydrostatic equation above.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>r<\/mi><\/msubsup><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>′<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>ρ<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m(r)=\\int_0^r4\\pi r^{\\prime2}\\rho\\,\\mathrm dr^\\prime=4\\pi\\rho r^3/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_c\\propto R^{-4}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\npressure increases by a factor of 16.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Spherical symmetry and quasi-static force balance without\nsubstantial acceleration.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>Rapidly evolving stages such as collapse, supernova explosions and\npulsations require time-dependent equations. Radiative and convective\nzones are schematic and differ with stellar mass and age. The\nconstant-density model is useful only for demonstrating the pressure\nscale and its\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^{-4}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndependence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Stellar structure couples mass, force and energy balances with a\nmaterial equation of state.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Compact Stars, Black Holes and\nAccretion<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/16-3-the-solar-interior-theory\">OpenStax – Astronomy 2e, §16.3 The Solar Interior: Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Hydrostatic balance and stellar interior, §16.3: hydrostatic balance; Subject relevance to lesson: M20-L3 – Stellar Structure, Hydrostatic Equilibrium and Evolution · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/21-thinking-ahead\">OpenStax – Astronomy 2e, Ch. 21 Thinking Ahead<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Stellar formation and evolution, chapter orientation: chapter overview on stellar evolution; Subject relevance to lesson: M20-L3 – Stellar Structure, Hydrostatic Equilibrium and Evolution · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M20-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M20-L4 – Compact Stars, Black Holes and Accretion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Bachelor: Astrophysics and Cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M9: two-body motion; M10: field\ntheory; M12: quantum states. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson: Stellar\nStructure, Hydrostatic Equilibrium and Evolution<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Estimate gravitational radii and\nelementary accretion power while distinguishing compact-object\ntypes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>The endpoints of stellar evolution can be extremely compact. White\ndwarfs are mainly supported by electron degeneracy pressure; the\nstructure of neutron stars is governed by dense nuclear matter. A black\nhole is not an ordinary body with a material surface. Its existence is\nnot inferred from darkness alone: orbital motion, emission from\nsurrounding material and dynamical measurements can reveal a highly\nconcentrated gravitational field. Luminous accreting matter lies\n<strong>outside<\/strong> the event horizon.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For a non-rotating, uncharged black hole, the Schwarzschild model\ngives\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_s=\\frac{2GM}{c^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nThis is the event-horizon radius, not the size of a glowing accretion\ndisc. An elementary accretion estimate uses\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mover><mi>M<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L\\approx\\eta\\dot M c^2,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>M<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the mass inflow rate and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na model-dependent efficiency. Emission from accretion does not prove\nmatter has crossed the event horizon. Relativistic dynamics are required\nclose to the compact object.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mo>⊙<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=10M_\\odot<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mo>⊙<\/mo><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.9885<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>30<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_\\odot\\approx1.9885\\times10^{30}\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe radius is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>29.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_s\\approx29.5\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a <strong>purely illustrative<\/strong> inflow\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>M<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot M=10^{10}\\,\\mathrm{kg/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe estimated power is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>9.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>25<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L\\approx9.0\\times10^{25}\\,\\mathrm W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nChanging\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges this power linearly. The figure depicts a disc and a\nnon-luminous central region; it is not a numerically computed spacetime\nimage.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_046_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic accretion disc around a dark central region indicating the event horizon.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic accretion disc around a dark\ncentral region indicating the event horizon.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Accretion environment and central horizon in a Schwarzschild\nteaching model; not to scale.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Estimate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a Schwarzschild black hole with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mo>⊙<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5M_\\odot<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>M<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles at constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does luminous surrounding matter not imply a bright horizon\nsurface?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>14.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_s\\approx14.8\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The model luminosity doubles.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Radiation is produced in hot matter outside the horizon.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>Rotation, magnetic fields, radiative transfer and feedback can\nmarkedly change real accretion luminosities. The Schwarzschild radius\napplies to an ideal non-rotating hole; neutron stars instead have\nmaterial surfaces and different equations of state. The illustration is\na teaching geometry, not an observational image.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Compact objects require distinguishing material surfaces, event\nhorizons and the observable surroundings.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Galaxy Dynamics, Rotation Curves\nand Dark Matter<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/24-6-evidence-for-black-holes\">OpenStax – Astronomy 2e, §24.6 Evidence for Black Holes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Indirect dynamical and accretion evidence, §24.6: dynamical and accretion evidence; Subject relevance to lesson: M20-L4 – Compact Stars, Black Holes and Accretion · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M20-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M20-L5 – Galaxy Dynamics, Rotation Curves and Dark Matter<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Bachelor: Astrophysics and Cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M3: circular motion; M9:\ngravitational potential; M16: measurement models. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson: Compact Stars,\nBlack Holes and Accretion<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Translate a rotation curve into\nan idealised mass model and identify its geometric limitations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Stars and gas clouds in spiral galaxies do not orbit a single\ndominant central mass in the same way as planets in a simple\nsolar-system model. Doppler observations can yield rotational speed as a\nfunction of distance from the galactic centre, subject to viewing\ngeometry. In many galaxies, outer rotation speeds remain higher than\nsimple models of the visible stars and gas would predict. A spatially\nextended gravitating component, often modelled as a dark-matter halo, is\na major explanation within the standard cosmological framework.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For a spherical mass distribution and circular orbits, force balance\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_c^2/r=GM(&lt;r)/r^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>G<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(&lt;r)=\\frac{v_c^2r}{G}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nIf enclosed mass became nearly constant at large radii, then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_c\\propto r^{-1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn approximately flat curve\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_c\\approx\\mathrm{const}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninstead corresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(&lt;r)\\propto r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin this spherical approximation. Galactic discs are not spherical: the\nformula is useful for scale estimates but is not an exact mass-recovery\nalgorithm.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>Take a synthetic rotation curve with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_c=200\\,\\mathrm{km/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=10\\,\\mathrm{kpc}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUsing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.0857<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>19<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{kpc}\\approx3.0857\\times10^{19}\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe spherical approximation gives\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.85<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>41<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>9.3<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mo>⊙<\/mo><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(&lt;10\\,\\mathrm{kpc})\\approx1.85\\times10^{41}\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\approx9.3\\times10^{10}M_\\odot.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nBoth plotted curves are synthetic. A declining baryonic-model curve and\nan outer flat total-speed curve illustrate the diagnostic principle, not\nmeasurements of a particular galaxy.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_109_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Synthetic galaxy rotation curves: flat outer total speed and declining visible-mass model.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Synthetic galaxy rotation curves: flat\nouter total speed and declining visible-mass model.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Schematic rotation curves for two mass models.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>No observational data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(&lt;r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nscale for a flat rotation curve?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(&lt;r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat half the speed and the same radius?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the spherical formula not exact for a thin disc?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In the spherical model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M(&lt;r)\\propto r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It falls to a quarter because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\propto v_c^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A disc generates a different spatial gravitational-field\ngeometry.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>Line-of-sight velocities require correction for galaxy inclination.\nNon-circular motions, gas pressure, warped discs, stellar populations,\ngas and mass-to-light ratios create further uncertainties. Rotation\ncurves provide strong evidence for a mass discrepancy under specified\ndynamics; they do not directly reveal the microscopic identity of dark\nmatter or logically settle every alternative dynamical model by\nthemselves.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>A rotation curve is a dynamical measurement; its mass interpretation\ndepends on geometry and modelling.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Exoplanets, Transit Photometry\nand Detection Limits<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/28-4-the-challenge-of-dark-matter\">OpenStax – Astronomy 2e, §28.4 The Challenge of Dark Matter<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Galaxy rotation and mass discrepancy, §28.4: rotation curves and mass discrepancy; Subject relevance to lesson: M20-L5 – Galaxy Dynamics, Rotation Curves and Dark Matter · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M20-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M20-L6 – Exoplanets, Transit Photometry and Detection Limits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Bachelor: Astrophysics and Cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7: optics; M9: Kepler motion; M16:\ntime series and signal-to-noise. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson: Galaxy\nDynamics, Rotation Curves and Dark Matter<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Convert a transit depth into a\nradius ratio and explain at least three detection biases or\nuncertainties.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>For a suitable line of sight, an orbiting planet crosses a stellar\ndisc and blocks part of its light, causing a periodic decrease in\nmeasured flux. A transit first measures a ratio of projected areas, not\nan absolute planetary radius. An independently inferred stellar radius\nis needed to obtain the planet’s radius. The radial-velocity method\ninstead tracks motion of the star about the system barycentre and\nconstrains a mass-dependent quantity. Combining both measurements\nconstrains the planet’s mean density.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>For a central transit across a uniformly bright stellar disc with a\nsmall planet, the relative flux reduction obeys\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mo>⋆<\/mo><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta\\approx\\left(\\frac{R_p}{R_\\star}\\right)^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mo>⋆<\/mo><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><msqrt><mi>δ<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_p/R_\\star\\approx\\sqrt{\\delta}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a nearly circular orbit and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≪<\/mo><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mo>⋆<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_p\\ll M_\\star<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nKepler’s third law yields\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mo>⋆<\/mo><\/msub><msup><mi>P<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^3\\approx\\frac{GM_\\star P^2}{4\\pi^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nTransit duration adds information about orbital geometry.\nRadial-velocity amplitudes typically constrain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_p\\sin i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalone; transit geometry also constrains orbital inclination\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>In a synthetic light curve, the normalised stellar flux decreases\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.000<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.990<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.990<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nduring a transit. Therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.010<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta=0.010<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mo>⋆<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_p/R_\\star=0.10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAssuming\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mo>⋆<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>700<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_\\star=700\\,000\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe estimated planet radius is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>70<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">70\\,000\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows an idealised central transit and its modelled dip in\nflux.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_085_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Star and transiting planet with a synthetic light curve showing a one-percent flux dip.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Star and transiting planet with a\nsynthetic light curve showing a one-percent flux dip.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Central planetary transit: the model light curve implies a radius\nratio of 0.10.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mo>⋆<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_p/R_\\star<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0004<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta=0.0004<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does transit depth change if planetary radius doubles at fixed\nstellar radius?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a single flux decrease not establish an exoplanet?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mo>⋆<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>0.0004<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_p/R_\\star=\\sqrt{0.0004}=0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Transit depth becomes four times larger.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Stellar variability, instrument artefacts and eclipsing binaries can\nmimic a dip; repeated transits and independent checks are needed.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>Limb darkening, grazing geometry, stellar spots and correlated noise\nmodify transit shapes. The method is geometrically selective and, at\nfixed data quality, favours larger planets and shorter periods.\nEclipsing binary stars can also mimic planetary signals. The graphic\ncontains only calculated teaching-model values and no survey mission\ndata.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Transits primarily constrain radius ratios; modelling and independent\nmethods are needed for robust characterisation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Cosmic Expansion, Redshift and\nBackground Radiation<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/21-4-planets-beyond-the-solar-system-search-and-discovery\">OpenStax – Astronomy 2e, §21.4 Planets beyond the Solar System: Search and Discovery<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Transit geometry and radial-velocity complement, §21.4: transit and radial-velocity techniques; Subject relevance to lesson: M20-L6 – Exoplanets, Transit Photometry and Detection Limits · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M20-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M20-L7 – Cosmic Expansion, Redshift and Background Radiation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Bachelor: Astrophysics and Cosmology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M10: electromagnetic waves; M13:\nblackbody statistics; M20-L1: distance ladder. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson: Exoplanets,\nTransit Photometry and Detection Limits<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Use small cosmological redshifts\nas approximate distance indicators and explain the cosmic microwave\nbackground.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Question and Observation<\/h2>\n<p>Light from distant galaxies reaches us after travelling through an\nevolving universe. Spectral features are observed at longer wavelengths\non average because the cosmic scale factor changes between emission and\nobservation. This is not simply an ordinary object moving through static\nspace. Another key observable is the nearly isotropic cosmic microwave\nbackground, with a remarkably thermal spectrum and small anisotropies.\nObservation, data reduction and cosmological interpretation must be kept\ndistinct.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical Model and Equations<\/h2>\n<p>Redshift is defined by\n<math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+z=\\frac{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{obs}}}{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{em}}}=\\frac{a(t_0)}{a(t_{\\mathrm{em}})}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nFor sufficiently small cosmological redshifts one may use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v\\approx cz<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nheuristically and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v\\approx H_0d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the local Hubble–Lemaître relation. Combining them gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\approx cz/H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nonly in the\nlow-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\napproximately linear regime. At high redshift the distance definition\ndepends on a cosmological expansion model. The cosmic microwave\nbackground closely resembles blackbody radiation with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.73<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\approx2.73\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example and Teaching Diagram<\/h2>\n<p>A hypothetical galaxy has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.023<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=0.023<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTaking an <strong>illustrative, not measured here<\/strong>, Hubble\nconstant of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>70<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0=70\\,\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}\\,Mpc^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6900<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">cz\\approx6900\\,\\mathrm{km/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>98.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\approx98.5\\,\\mathrm{Mpc}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is not a precise distance determination and ignores peculiar\nmotions. The illustration shows a synthetic linear relation and a\nqualitative thermal microwave spectrum associated with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.73<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.73\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_086_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Synthetic linear Hubble diagram and idealised thermal microwave spectrum.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Synthetic linear Hubble diagram and\nidealised thermal microwave spectrum.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Low-redshift Hubble approximation and schematic background\nspectrum.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-Assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{\\mathrm{em}}=500\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>550<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{\\mathrm{obs}}=550\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\approx cz/H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.010<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=0.010<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>70<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0=70\\,\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}\\,Mpc^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=cz<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot be treated as an ordinary relative speed at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z\\sim1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>550<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>500<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=550/500-1=0.10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2998<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>70<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>42.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\approx(2998/70)\\,\\mathrm{Mpc}\\approx42.8\\,\\mathrm{Mpc}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Because large cosmological redshifts require a time-dependent\nexpansion model.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Validity Limits and Quality<\/h2>\n<p>The chosen Hubble constant is a rounded teaching value, not a claim\nabout the currently preferred measured value. Peculiar motions affect\nthe low-distance approximation. The background spectrum alone does not\nprovide a model-free current age of the universe; parameter inference\nrequires a jointly fitted theory and complementary observations. The\nplotted curve is synthetic, not a reproduction of an observational\ncatalogue.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Cosmological redshift and the microwave background are central\nobservables, interpreted through an explicit model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/29-thinking-ahead\">OpenStax – Astronomy 2e, Ch. 29 Thinking Ahead<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Cosmological expansion and background, chapter orientation: chapter overview of expansion; Subject relevance to lesson: M20-L7 – Cosmic Expansion, Redshift and Background Radiation · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/astronomy-2e/pages/29-4-the-cosmic-microwave-background\">OpenStax – Astronomy 2e, §29.4 The Cosmic Microwave Background<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · CMB blackbody spectrum and temperature, §29.4: microwave-background blackbody spectrum; Subject relevance to lesson: M20-L7 – Cosmic Expansion, Redshift and Background Radiation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
